WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.292
Date parsing is a good place to be conservative.

00:00:03.292 --> 00:00:07.032
In the project, we support two known formats.

00:00:07.032 --> 00:00:13.242
The first is year, month, day, such as 2026 dash 10 dash 22.

00:00:13.242 --> 00:00:19.312
When the source matches that structure, we convert it to a date.

00:00:19.312 --> 00:00:23.432
The second is month, day, year, using slashes.

00:00:23.432 --> 00:00:28.359
When the text matches that format, we use the documented conversion rule.

00:00:28.832 --> 00:00:34.970
What happens when the source value uses some other format or contains an impossible date?

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:38.295
We return null rather than inventing a date.

00:00:38.295 --> 00:00:44.030
For example, if the source contains text that could be interpreted in two different ways,

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:47.330
guessing could silently create the wrong date.

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:53.180
A null cleaned date plus a review flag is safer than a confident but incorrect value.
